    id: "f4fe5ba46a4845e8f243",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Israel returns 22 stolen ancient artefacts to Greece",
    summary: "The cultural exchange comes amid strengthening ties between the two countries.",
    preview: "The cultural exchange comes amid strengthening ties between the two countries. Israel has returned 22 ancient artefacts stolen from Greece, a cultural exchange that comes as the two countries strengthen ties. Greek and Israeli officials did not provide details on Tuesday on how the artefacts were discovered in Israel or the circumstances surrounding their illegal removal from Greece.",
    body: "The cultural exchange comes amid strengthening ties between the two countries.\n\nIsrael has returned 22 ancient artefacts stolen from Greece, a cultural exchange that comes as the two countries strengthen ties.\n\nGreek and Israeli officials did not provide details on Tuesday on how the artefacts were discovered in Israel or the circumstances surrounding their illegal removal from Greece.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3Air France says it will follow Israeli rule barring crew from some nations\n\n- list 2 of 3Israel’s Yellow Line in Gaza advances, leaving displaced with nowhere to go\n\n- list 3 of 3Israeli attacks kill one in Gaza, wound journalists in occupied West Bank\n\nThe Israel Antiquities Authority’s Theft Prevention Unit had located the items, Pninat Yanay, Israel’s ambassador to Greece, said.\n\nGreek Minister of Culture Lina Mendoni accepted the objects from the Israeli Antiquities Authority at a ceremony held at the National Archaeological Museum in Athens.\n\n“It is the first time that Israel has returned antiquities to Greece, marking the starting point of closer cooperation between the two countries for the protection of cultural heritage,” Mendoni said.\n\n“Every return of a cultural object to its place of origin has a broader symbolic and moral dimension.”\n\nThe collection includes 15 Attic vases, six Corinthian vases, and a bronze female statuette.\n\nThe objects are dated between 700 BC and 320 BC.\n\nYanay said the return of the antiquities created “the conditions for further strengthening cooperation between Greece and Israel”.\n\nGreece-Israel relations\n\nGreece and Israel have strengthened energy and defence ties in recent years.\n\nIn August, Israel signed a $3.5bn defence deal to build a multi-layered air defence system in Greece, called the Achilles Shield. It is the largest defence deal between the two countries, and one of the most significant export agreements in Israel’s recent history.",
