    id: "e019f6d6282ef289c2d5",
    cat: "conflitti",
    title: "Ukrainian ex-officer goes on trial in Germany over Nord Stream blasts",
    summary: "The trial is expected to run into February and could test Germany's political stance on Ukraine.",
    preview: "The trial is expected to run into February and could test Germany’s political stance on Ukraine. A Ukrainian former military officer is standing trial in Germany, accused of organising the secret operation that blew up the Nord Stream gas pipelines in 2022 and cut Western Europe off from its biggest source of Russian natural gas. The trial opened on Wednesday at the Hamburg Higher Regional Court and is expected to run into February next year.",
    body: "The trial is expected to run into February and could test Germany’s political stance on Ukraine.\n\nA Ukrainian former military officer is standing trial in Germany, accused of organising the secret operation that blew up the Nord Stream gas pipelines in 2022 and cut Western Europe off from its biggest source of Russian natural gas.\n\nThe trial opened on Wednesday at the Hamburg Higher Regional Court and is expected to run into February next year.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 3 items\n\n- list 1 of 3German prosecutors charge Ukrainian suspect over Nord Stream explosions\n\n- list 2 of 3Why the world should care about the far right’s rise in Germany\n\n- list 3 of 3Ukrainian suspect, a ‘trained scuba diver’, arrested over Nord Stream blast\n\nIt is the first public hearing of the evidence in a years-long investigation.\n\nThe defendant, a 50-year-old identified under German privacy rules only as Serhii K., is accused of leading a group of Ukrainian agents – including divers and an explosives expert – in one of the biggest infrastructure sabotage operations of modern times.\n\nHe denies any involvement and is the only person on trial.\n\nThe prosecution\n\nSerhii K. was arrested in Italy in August 2025 and extradited to Germany that November.\n\nProsecutors accused him of working with other members of the military to plan the operation on behalf of unidentified Ukrainian state entities.\n\nThey allege he travelled to Germany through Poland on a forged Ukrainian passport about three weeks before the attack.\n\nThey say the group used a sailboat hired in the northern city of Rostock to reach an area near the Danish island of Bornholm, where they attached military-grade explosives with timers to the pipelines on the seabed.\n\nGerman prosecutors say attacking civilian targets and destroying infrastructure in this way would amount to a war crime.\n\nThe blasts and the fallout\n\nOn September 26, 2022, Swedish seismologists detected several blasts, some 17 hours apart.\n\nThree of the four concrete-coated steel pipelines ruptured, sending huge plumes of methane into the atmosphere.\n\nNord Stream 1 had supplied about half of Germany’s natural gas imports. Its twin, Nord Stream 2, was built but never opened, after Berlin cancelled approval shortly before Russia invaded Ukraine.\n\nMoscow denied blowing up its own pipelines and had already cut supplies in response to European sanctions.\n\nUkraine’s government denies involvement.\n\nRussia said on Monday it expected a “just verdict” naming the perpetrators and those who ordered the operation.\n\nAnother suspect, Vladimir Z., a trained scuba diver who prosecutors say was one of the divers on the team, was arrested in Croatia in August.\n\nLast month, a Croatian court approved handing over the wanted man to Germany.\n\nThe Nord Stream case has strained ties between Berlin and Kyiv, even as Germany remains one of Ukraine’s strongest backers, with Russia and Ukraine still trading strikes.",
