    cat: "economia-tech",
    title: "Singapore’s economy grows more than 5 percent amid AI boom",
    summary: "City-state's GDP expands 5.4 percent in July-September quarter, according to advance government estimates.",
    preview: "City-state’s GDP expands 5.4 percent in July-September quarter, according to advance government estimates. Singapore’s economy grew more than 5 percent in the most recent quarter, continuing an AI-fuelled boom that has made the city-state one of the fastest-growing advanced economies. Gross domestic product (GDP) grew 5.4 percent year-on-year between July and September, down from a 6.2 percent expansion in the second quarter, according to advance government estimates released on Wednesday.",
    body: "City-state’s GDP expands 5.4 percent in July-September quarter, according to advance government estimates.\n\nSingapore’s economy grew more than 5 percent in the most recent quarter, continuing an AI-fuelled boom that has made the city-state one of the fastest-growing advanced economies.\n\nGross domestic product (GDP) grew 5.4 percent year-on-year between July and September, down from a 6.2 percent expansion in the second quarter, according to advance government estimates released on Wednesday.\n\nRecommended Stories\n\nlist of 4 items\n\n- list 1 of 4UN Human Rights Council elects 15 members in uncontested vote\n\n- list 2 of 4Ukrainian ex-officer goes on trial in Germany over Nord Stream blasts\n\n- list 3 of 4Houthi missiles intercepted over Saudi Arabia as battles intensify\n\n- list 4 of 4Israel returns 22 stolen ancient artefacts to Greece\n\nManufacturing, which is dominated by the city-state’s semiconductor industry, grew 7.5 percent, according to the estimates from the Ministry of Trade and Industry.\n\nIncreased output in “electronics, precision engineering, transport engineering and general manufacturing clusters” drove growth, the ministry said, offsetting declines in the chemicals and biomedical sectors.\n\nConstruction, including AI-related investments such as data centres, expanded 8.8 per cent, according to the figures.\n\nDespite the heady expansion, Singapore’s central bank left its monetary policy settings untouched in its latest announcement on Wednesday, citing expectations that AI demand will cool in 2027.\n\nThe Monetary Authority of Singapore, which manages monetary policy through currency exchange rates rather than interest rates, said it expected core inflation to average 1.5–2.0 percent in 2026.\n\n“Global AI-driven growth is expected to ease in the latter half of next year from its current exceptional pace, while higher interest rates, amid elevated inflation in the advanced economies, would weigh on final demand growth,” the central bank said in a statement.\n\nSingapore is a major global player in the booming chip sector, a position that has helped it weather choppy economic conditions, including the energy shock prompted by the US-Israel war on Iran.\n\nThe Southeast Asian city-state accounts for roughly 10 percent of global semiconductor output and 20 percent of semiconductor equipment production, according to government figures.\n\nYeow Hwee Chua, an assistant professor of economics at Nanyang Technological University, described Singapore’s economic performance as “impressive for a high-income economy, especially given the uncertain global environment”.\n\n“The global technology investment boom has been an important driver,” Chua told Al Jazeera.\n\n“Strong demand for electronics has supported manufacturing and related wholesale trade, illustrating how Singapore benefits from its position in global supply chains,” he said.\n\nSingapore’s economy is expected to grow 5 percent in 2026, according to the IMF’s latest economic forecast, faster than any other advanced economy except Taiwan.\n\nThe city-state’s GDP grew 5 percent in 2025, its second straight year of growth above 5 percent – the first such streak since 2010 and 2011.",
